.TH KAGI-RUN 1 "September 2026" "Kagi 0.38.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kagi-run \- launch a command with explicit Linux scheduling and CPU affinity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kagi-run
[--policy normal|round-robin|fifo] [--priority N] [--cpus 2,3] -- COMMAND [ARGUMENTS]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Applies and verifies scheduling before replacing itself with COMMAND. Normal is the default and requires priority zero. Round-robin and FIFO require priority 1 through 99 and appropriate operating-system permission. Failure prevents COMMAND from starting.
.PP
Real-time policies must exclude at least one allowed logical CPU from their affinity. Without --cpus, the first allowed logical CPU is excluded automatically. This is not a hard real-time guarantee. Worker threads and child processes inherit scheduling unless they change it.
.SH SEE ALSO
sched(7), kagi-config(1)
